WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:08.550
<v Speaker 1>So today on our deep dive, we are trying to decode what is basically a medieval operating system for the physical universe.

2
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:11.310
<v Speaker 1>I mean, we're looking at a really huge stack of sources here.

3
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.150
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it is quite a lot of ground to cover.

4
00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:13.710
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

5
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:26.030
<v Speaker 1>We've got early Fatimid cosmology, some intense legal treatises, and really clear modern analyses from scholars like Henry Corbin, Farhad Daftry, and Khalil Andani.

6
00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:30.830
<v Speaker 1>We are stepping into a worldview where time and space are literal languages.

7
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.110
<v Speaker 1>So let's just jump straight in.

8
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:32.670
<v Speaker 2>Welcome.

9
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:41.310
<v Speaker 2>We're with the master and the disciple the moment the scholar names the seven speaker prophets and shows outer and inner as the body and spirit of religion, each living only through the other.

10
00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.790
<v Speaker 2>We really need to lay down the absolute core thesis here immediately.

11
00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.550
<v Speaker 2>The inner is the true religion.

12
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:53.310
<v Speaker 2>The outer is its revealed paths and symbols, body and spirit, each subsisting only through the other.

13
00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.990
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, I mean, that is just a massive statement to start out with.

14
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:56.750
<v Speaker 2>It really is.

15
00:00:56.830 --> 00:01:01.150
<v Speaker 1>And to understand why that thesis is so important, we have to look at the crisis happening in the text itself.

16
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.270
<v Speaker 1>We have the student, the seeker, who comes to his teacher, the elder.

17
00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.190
<v Speaker 2>He is in a lot of distress, actually.

18
00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:06.830
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

19
00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.830
<v Speaker 1>He is having this very specific agonizing crisis of faith.

20
00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.710
<v Speaker 1>He looks around at the physical earth, the changing seasons, the sky, the oceans, all the things he believes God made beautifully.

21
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.750
<v Speaker 1>But his religious authorities seemed totally hostile to it all.

22
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.510
<v Speaker 2>He seemed to resent the physical world.

23
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.150
<v Speaker 1>Yes.

24
00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:30.750
<v Speaker 1>And he asks the teacher, you know, why did the pious constantly condemn this lower world?

25
00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:36.830
<v Speaker 1>Why is spirituality always pitched as like a way of escaping the physical reality the creator built?

26
00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.190
<v Speaker 2>And that is a completely valid theological tension.

27
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:48.990
<v Speaker 2>I mean, if you hold a foundational belief that creation is deliberate, then looking at the physical results of that creation with disgust is just a huge contradiction.

28
00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:50.110
<v Speaker 1>It doesn't make any sense.

29
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:50.750
<v Speaker 2>No, it doesn't.

30
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:56.750
<v Speaker 2>And when faced with that contradiction, the first answer that a lot of religious philosophers historically reached for was pure allegorism.

31
00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:57.950
<v Speaker 1>Meaning what exactly?

32
00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:08.830
<v Speaker 2>Meaning they would try to solve the tension by arguing that the physical world and all the rigorous physical rituals of religion aren't actually real or binding.

33
00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:14.750
<v Speaker 2>They would claim these things are simply loose metaphors for a higher detached spiritual truth.

34
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.270
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay.

35
00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.990
<v Speaker 1>How is this different from any allegorists' trick?

36
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:26.950
<v Speaker 1>Because, I mean, if you just turn everything into a vague metaphor, you can make a sacred text or the physical world mean absolutely whatever is convenient for you.

37
00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:27.670
<v Speaker 2>Absolutely.

38
00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:33.070
<v Speaker 1>It just feels like a dodge to avoid dealing with the actual dirt, the stone, and the rigid laws of the world.

39
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.550
<v Speaker 2>Well, the scholar in the text agrees with you entirely.

40
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:41.150
<v Speaker 2>He does not offer the student that cheap allegorical dodge where the physical realm is just casually dismissed.

41
00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:48.270
<v Speaker 2>Instead, the teacher answers the crisis by revealing a massive, objective mathematical architecture.

42
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.470
<v Speaker 1>Okay, so he gets structural.

43
00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:50.590
<v Speaker 2>Very structural.

44
00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:55.230
<v Speaker 2>Remember, this text originates perfectly in the intellectual climate of the Fatimids.

45
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.110
<v Speaker 2>They were an intellectual powerhouse in the 10th century.

46
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.270
<v Speaker 2>They weren't just collecting poetic metaphors.

47
00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.790
<v Speaker 2>Their cosmology was a strict science of spiritual reality.

48
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:11.390
<v Speaker 2>To prove this to the disciple, the elder introduces the foundational lineage of history, the seven speaker prophets.

49
00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.710
<v Speaker 1>So these would be the major messengers who institute the divine law throughout human history.

50
00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:26.430
<v Speaker 1>The text names them systematically as Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, Muhammad, peace, and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and finally the awaited one.

51
00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.790
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, that exact list is the absolute load-bearing skeleton of this entire tradition.

52
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.270
<v Speaker 2>the modern scholar Farhad Daftari.

53
00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:39.550
<v Speaker 2>He traces this specific sequence through all the major theologians of that era.

54
00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.270
<v Speaker 1>It is remarkably consistent across all their work.

55
00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:42.830
<v Speaker 2>It is.

56
00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:46.270
<v Speaker 2>And Henry Corbin pointed out a truly fascinating element of this structure.

57
00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.870
<v Speaker 2>He noted that in this framework, time itself is shaped like an alphabet.

58
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.710
<v Speaker 1>Wait, shaped like an alphabet?

59
00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:52.350
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

60
00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.830
<v Speaker 2>Each of those seven heralds is a vital letter.

61
00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:55.950
<v Speaker 2>They aren't just

62
00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:59.150
<v Speaker 2>like historical figures who lived and died in the past.

63
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.750
<v Speaker 2>They are the structural pillars holding up the spiritual universe.

64
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.710
<v Speaker 1>So the elder is essentially handing the student a blueprint.

65
00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:12.430
<v Speaker 1>But if time is an alphabet, how does that actually map onto the physical sky that the student is looking up at?

66
